"Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.22636)\nciteturn3academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Title: Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures\nTitle: Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures\nAuthors: João Miguel Machado, João P. G. Ramos\nDate: Thu Nov 27 17:14:52 2025\n\nBy employing the recently obtained sharp stability versions of the Prékopa--Leindler inequality, we are able to obtain a sharp quantitative stability version for the Brascamp--Lieb inequality, as well as several different results on the stability of moment measures. As main features of our results, we highlight the independence of the stability constant for the Brascamp--Lieb inequality on the convex function considered, a completely novel feature. In the realm of moment measures, we highlight in the same vein that the stability results obtained are uniform, which we expect to be particularly valuable not only from a purely mathematical point of view, but also for applications.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Boundary Measures for Geometric Inference (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220104373_Boundary_Measures_for_Geometric_Inference)\nciteturn3search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Article PDF Available ... The main contribution of this work is the proof of a quantitative stability theorem for boundary measures using tools of convex analysis and geometric measure theory. ... Frédéric Chazal ·David Cohen-Steiner · ... e-mail: Quentin.Merigot@sophia.inria.fr ... More recent works of Chazal et al. [5,6] studied geometric inference via point cloud approximations to boundary measures using Monte Carlo methods.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Boundary Measures for Geometric Inference\n\n  * December 2009\n  * Foundations of Computational Mathematics 10(2):221-240\n\nDOI:10.1007/s10208-009-9056-2\n\n  * Source\n  * DBLP\n\nAuthors:\n\nFrédéric Chazal\n\n  * National Institute for Research in Computer Science and Control\n\nDavid Cohen-Steiner\n\n  * National Institute for Research in Computer Science and Control\n\nQuentin Mérigot\n\nQuentin Mérigot\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nWe study the boundary measures of compact subsets of the d-dimensional Euclidean space, which are closely related to Federer’s curvature measures. We show that they can be computed efficiently for point clouds and suggest that these measures can be used for geometric inference. The main contribution of this work is the proof of a quantitative stability theorem for boundary measures using tools of convex analysis and geometric measure theory. As a corollary we obtain a stability result for Federer’s curvature measures of a compact set, showing that they can be reliably estimated from point-cloud approximations.\n\nBoundary measure for a sampled mechanical part… \n\nFigures - uploaded by David Cohen-Steiner\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll figure content in this area was uploaded by David Cohen-Steiner\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-texts 2\n\n0fcfd50b6842729d310000\n\n00.pdf\n\nContent uploaded by David Cohen-Steiner\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by David Cohen-Steiner\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\n0706.21\n\n53.pdf\n\nContent uploaded by David Cohen-Steiner\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by David Cohen-Steiner\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nFound Comput Math\n\nDOI 10.1007/s10208-009-9056-2\n\nBoundary Measures for Geometric Inference\n\nFrédéric Chazal ·David Cohen-Steiner ·\n\nQuentin Mérigot\n\nReceived: 6 February 2009 / Revised: 29 October 2009 / Accepted: 29 October 2009\n\n© SFoCM 2009\n\nAbstract We study the boundary measures of compact subsets of the d-dimensional\n\nEuclidean space, which are closely related to Federer’s curvature measures.Weshow\n\nthat they can be computed efﬁciently for point clouds and suggest that these measures\n\ncan be used for geometric inference. The main contribution of this work is the proof of\n\na quantitative stability theorem for boundary measures using tools of convex analysis\n\nand geometric measure theory. As a corollary we obtain a stability result for Federer’s\n\ncurvature measures of a compact set, showing that they can be reliably estimated from\n\npoint-cloud approximations.\n\nKeywords Geometric inference ·Curvature measures ·Convex functions ·Nearest\n\nneighbors ·Point clouds\n\nMathematics Subject Classiﬁcation (2000) 52A39 ·51K10 ·49Q15\n\n1 Introduction\n\nRecently there has been a growing interest in applying geometric methods to data\n\nanalysis. This approach uses well-known geometric or topological properties such as\n\nCommunicated by Herbert Edelsbrunner.\n\nF. Chazal\n\nGeometrica, INRIA Saclay, Orsay, France\n\nD. Cohen-Steiner ·Q. Mérigot ()\n\nGeometrica, INRIA Sophia-Antipolis, Sophia-Antipolis, France\n\ne-mail: Quentin.Merigot@sophia.inria.fr\n\nFound Comput Math\n\ncurvature and intrinsic dimension in order to describe and understand the structure of\n\ndata represented by high dimensional point clouds.\n\nThe general problem of geometric inference can be stated as follows: given a noisy\n\npoint-cloud approximation C of a compact set K⊆R d, how can we recover geomet-\n\nric and topological information about K, such as its curvature, sharp edges, bound-\n\naries, Betti numbers, or Euler–Poincaré characteristic, etc., knowing only the point\n\ncloud C?\n\nPrevious Work For smooth surfaces embedded in R 3, inference problems are by\n\nnow well mastered. In particular, several algorithms (e.g. [2]) allow us to reconstruct\n\na topologically correct and geometrically close piecewise-linear approximation from\n\na sufﬁciently dense point cloud. From these reconstructions, differential quantities\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFound Comput Math (https://geometrica.saclay.inria.fr/team/Fred.Chazal/papers/JFOCMboundaryMeasure.pdf)\nciteturn3search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.9 years ago; Crawled: 16.9 years ago; Frédéric Chazal · David Cohen-Steiner · Quentin Mérigot ... The main contribution of this work is the proof of a quantitative stability theorem for boundary measures using tools of convex analysis and geometric measure theory. ... e-mail: Quentin.Merigot@sophia.inria.fr\nFound Comput Math  \nDOI 10.1007/s10208-009-9056-2\n\nFOUNDATIONS OF COMPUTATIONAL MATHEMATICS  \nThe Journal of the Society for the Foundations of Computational Mathematics\n\nBoundary Measures for Geometric Inference\n\nFrédéric Chazal · David Cohen-Steiner · Quentin Mérigot\n\nReceived: 6 February 2009 / Revised: 29 October 2009 / Accepted: 29 October 2009  \n© SFoCM 2009\n\n**Abstract** We study the *boundary measures* of compact subsets of the *d*-dimensional Euclidean space, which are closely related to Federer’s *curvature measures*. We show that they can be computed efficiently for point clouds and suggest that these measures can be used for geometric inference. The main contribution of this work is the proof of a quantitative stability theorem for boundary measures using tools of convex analysis and geometric measure theory. As a corollary we obtain a stability result for Federer’s curvature measures of a compact set, showing that they can be reliably estimated from point-cloud approximations.\n\n**Keywords** Geometric inference · Curvature measures · Convex functions · Nearest neighbors · Point clouds\n\n**Mathematics Subject Classification (2000)** 52A39 · 51K10 · 49Q15\n\n**1 Introduction**\n\nRecently there has been a growing interest in applying geometric methods to data analysis. This approach uses well-known geometric or topological properties such as\n\nCommunicated by Herbert Edelsbrunner.\n\nF. Chazal  \nGeometrica, INRIA Saclay, Orsay, France\n\nD. Cohen-Steiner · Q. Mérigot (✉)  \nGeometrica, INRIA Sophia-Antipolis, Sophia-Antipolis, France  \ne-mail: Quentin.Merigot@sophia.inria.fr\n\nPublished online: 08 December 2009\n\nSpringer--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n6 F. Chazal, D. Cohen-Steiner, A. Lieutier, Q. Mérigot, B. Thibert (https://geometrica.saclay.inria.fr/team/Fred.Chazal/papers/ccslmt-icutn-17/inference.pdf)\nciteturn3search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.8 years ago; Chazal, D.Cohen-Steiner, A. ... ## 2 Boundary measures and Federer's curvature measures ... Loosely speaking, the boundary measure associated to a surface encodes the distribution of normals to the surface *at a certain scale*, and can be used to detect sharp edges, or highly curved areas, where the concentration of normals is much higher.\n6 F. Chazal, D. Cohen-Steiner, A. Lieutier, Q. Mérigot, B. Thibert\n\n\\((r,t)\\)-double offset of \\(K\\) is the set of points that are at distance \\(t\\) of the complementary of \\(K^r\\):\n\\[\nK^{r,t} := \\mathbb{R}^d \\setminus \\overline{K^r_t} = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^d : d(x,\\mathbb{R}^d \\setminus K^r) \\le t\\}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThe following theorem is thus a direct consequence [6].\n\n**Theorem 2 (Double offset theorem).** Let \\(K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d\\) be a set with positive \\(\\mu\\)-reach. Then, for any radius \\(r\\) in \\((0,\\mathrm{reach}_\\mu(K))\\) and every \\(t < \\mu r\\) the hypersurface \\(\\partial K^{r,t}\\) is \\(C^{1,1}\\)-smooth. In addition,\n\\[\n\\mathrm{reach}(\\partial K^{r,t}) \\ge \\min(t,\\mu r - t).\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThis implies in particular that the smallest of the principal radii of curvature at any point of \\(\\partial K^{r,t}\\) is at least \\(\\min(t,\\mu r - t)\\).\n\n## 2 Boundary measures and Federer's curvature measures\n\nIn this section, we introduce Federer's curvature measures, which apply to a large class of compact subsets embedded in the Euclidean space. The main objective here is to prove a stability theorem for these curvature measures which implies that it is possible to estimate them from point cloud data. As a first step, we consider a a simpler notion called *boundary measures* and introduced in [8] for the purpose of geometric inference.\n\n### 2.1 Boundary measures\n\nLoosely speaking, the boundary measure associated to a surface encodes the distribution of normals to the surface *at a certain scale*, and can be used to detect sharp edges, or highly curved areas, where the concentration of normals is much higher.\n\n**Definition 8 (Boundary measures).** If \\(K\\) is a compact subset and \\(E\\) a domain of \\(\\mathbb{R}^d\\), the *boundary measure* \\(\\mu_{K,E}\\) is defined as follows: for any subset \\(B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d\\), \\(\\mu_{K,E}(B)\\) is the \\(d\\)-volume of the set of points of \\(E\\) whose projection on \\(K\\) is in \\(B\\), i.e.\n\\[\n\\mu_{K,E}(B) = \\mathcal{H}^d\\!\\left(p_K^{-1}(B \\cap K)\\cap E\\right).\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nHere and in the following, \\(\\mathcal{H}^d\\) denotes the \\(d\\)-dimensional Hausdorff measure. By construction, the total mass of this measure is equal to \\(\\mathcal{H}^d(E)\\).\n\n*Example 4.* Let \\(S\\) be a unit-length segment in the plane with endpoints \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). The set \\(S^r\\) is the union of a rectangle of dimension \\(1 \\times 2r\\) whose points project on the segment and two half-disks of radius \\(r\\) whose points are projected on \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). It follows that\n\\[\n\\mu_{S,S^r} = 2r\\,\\mathcal{H}^1|_S + \\frac{\\pi}{2}r^2\\delta_a + \\frac{\\pi}{2}r^2\\delta_b\n\\tag{14}\n\\]--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantitative stability for the Brascamp–Lieb inequality and moment measures (1 juillet 2026) · Service de gestion de conférences de Mathrice (Indico) (https://indico.math.cnrs.fr/event/16902/?print=1)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Quantitative stability for the Brascamp–Lieb inequality and moment measures ... In this talk, we present recent results on quantitative stability for the Brascamp–Lieb inequality, around the variance with respect to a log-concave measure.\n\n# Quantitative stability for the Brascamp–Lieb inequality and moment measures\n\n## par Joao Machado (LMCRC Huawei)\n\nmercredi 1 juil. 2026, 10:30 → 11:30 Europe/Paris\n\n112 (Braconnier)\n\nDescription\n\nIn this talk, we present recent results on quantitative stability for the Brascamp–Lieb inequality, around the variance with respect to a log-concave measure. Several proofs of this inequality are known in the literature. Classical approaches rely in particular on the Bakry–Émery Γ-calculus, while others, such as that of Bobkov and Ledoux (2000), are based on the Prékopa–Leindler inequality.\n\nWe explain how the optimal quantitative stability version of the Prékopa–Leindler inequality obtained by Figalli, van Hintum, and Tiba (2025) can be combined with the theory of moment measures to strengthen the Bobkov–Ledoux proof strategy. This approach yields a quantitative stability version of the Brascamp–Lieb inequality with several remarkable properties: optimality of the stability exponent and a constant depending only on the dimension.\n\nIn particular, this constant is universal over the class of log-concave measures with essentially continuous convex potentials. This minimal regularity assumption on the convex potential is optimal to ensure non-degeneracy of the associated moment measure, as shown by Cordero-Erausquin and Klartag (2015). Finally, we discuss how this result can be used to understand quantitative stability for moment measures themselves, highlighting the close links between these two problems.\n\nThis is joint work with João Pedro Ramos: https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.22636--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStability of boundary measures F. Chazal D. Cohen- (https://www.lix.polytechnique.fr/Labo/Frank.Nielsen/seminars/FredericChazal-measures.pdf)\nciteturn3search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Chazal, D.Cohen-Steiner, Q.Mérigot | Stability of boundary measures --- | ---\nWasserstein distance Assume measures µ and ν are discrete : µ = P c δ , ν = P d δ i i xi j j yj we suppose that mass(µ) = mass(ν) a transport plan between is a set of nonnegative coefficients p specifying the amount of mass ij which is transported from x to y j, with i Xp = d and Xp = c ij j ij i i j the cost of a transport plan is C(p) = P ∥x −y j∥p ij i ij W (µ, ν) = inf C(p) p geom-logo F. Chazal, D. Cohen-Steiner, Q. Mérigot | Stability of boundary measures --- | -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStability of boundary measures (https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.2153)\nciteturn3academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.3 years ago; Authors: Frédéric Chazal, David Cohen-Steiner, Quentin Mérigot ... The main contribution of this work is the proof a quantitative stability theorem for boundary measures using tools of convex analysis and geometric measure theory.\nTitle: Stability of boundary measures\nAuthors: Frédéric Chazal, David Cohen-Steiner, Quentin Mérigot\nDate: Thu Jun 14 16:03:28 2007\n\nWe introduce the boundary measure at scale r of a compact subset of the n-dimensional Euclidean space. We show how it can be computed for point clouds and suggest these measures can be used for feature detection. The main contribution of this work is the proof a quantitative stability theorem for boundary measures using tools of convex analysis and geometric measure theory. As a corollary we obtain a stability result for Federer's curvature measures of a compact, allowing to compute them from point-cloud approximations of the compact.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2511.22636] Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures (https://owu.terracat.net/abs/2511.22636?__owu_origin_v1=aHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmc)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last month; > Abstract:We develop a quantitative stability theory for moment measures based on a new sharp uniform stability principle for the Brascamp-Lieb variance inequality in terms of the $L^1$-distance. ... This uniformity is absent from previous results in the literature and, beyond its intrinsic mathematical interest, it is the mechanism that allows stability of the Brascamp-Lieb inequality to transfer to nonlinear variational problems such as the moment-measure problem.\n\n# Mathematics > Functional Analysis\n\n[Submitted on 27 Nov 2025 (v1), last revised 29 Jul 2026 (this version, v3)]\n\n# Title:Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures\n\nAuthors:João Miguel Machado, João P.G. Ramos\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures, by Jo\\~ao Miguel Machado and Jo\\~ao P.G. Ramos\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:We develop a quantitative stability theory for moment measures based on a new sharp uniform stability principle for the Brascamp-Lieb variance inequality in terms of the $L^1$-distance. Our results yield structural stability estimates for solutions of the moment-measure problem that are uniform over a natural class of convex functions, thereby addressing several questions that have been open in this direction.\n> A key novelty of our approach is that the Brascamp-Lieb stability bound is not only sharp in its stability exponent, but also uniform across a broad class of convex potentials. This uniformity is absent from previous results in the literature and, beyond its intrinsic mathematical interest, it is the mechanism that allows stability of the Brascamp-Lieb inequality to transfer to nonlinear variational problems such as the moment-measure problem. We moreover show that the $L^1-$nature of this stability estimate is sharp, in the sense that such a uniform estimate cannot hold in any $L^p$-metric, with $p>1$.\n\nSubjects:  | Functional Analysis (math.FA)\nMSC (Mathematical Subject Classification) classes:  | 39B82, 26D10, 49Q22\nCite as:  | arXiv:2511.22636 [math.FA]\n   | (or arXiv:2511.22636v3 [math.FA] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.22636 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: João Miguel Machado [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 27 Nov 2025 17:14:52 UTC (40 KB)\n[v2] Tue, 3 Mar 2026 15:59:57 UTC (43 KB)\n[v3] Wed, 29 Jul 2026 00:25:21 UTC (52 KB)\n\nFull-text links:\n\n## Access Paper:\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures, by Jo\\~ao Miguel Machado and Jo\\~ao P.G. Ramos\n\n  * View PDF\n  * HTML (experimental)\n  * TeX Source\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDétection de structure géométrique dans les nuages de points (https://theses.hal.science/tel-00443038/preview/these-merigot.pdf)\nciteturn3search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; We introduce a notion of distance function to a probability measure, that is ... Mérigot. ... — Boundary measures for geometric inference., F.Chazal, D.Cohen-Steiner,\nGENERAL INTRODUCTION\n15\nVCM in an efﬁcient way in 3D, and study the methods experimentally by\napplying them on various point cloud models under noise and strong sampling\nbias.\nCHAPTER IV\nWe introduce a notion of distance function to a probability measure, that is\nstable under Wasserstein approximation. This distance-to-measure function\nnaturally associates a geometry and a topology to every probability measures\non Rd, through the sublevel sets of the function. We show that the distance-\nto-measure functions shares all features of the usual distance functions (such\nas semiconcavity) which are used in the proof of many geometric inference\nresults. This allows to extend, sometimes almost verbatim, these results to the\nnew setting. Finally, this new setting raises some interesting computational\nissues, which we discuss.\nPublications\n— Size of the medial axis and Federer’s curvature measures., Q. Mérigot.\n(submitted)\n— Boundary measures for geometric inference., F. Chazal, D. Cohen-Steiner,\nand Q. Mérigot. Found Comput Math 10, 221-240 (2010)\n— Robust Voronoi-based Curvature and Feature Estimation, Q. Mérigot, M.\nOvsjanikov, and L. Guibas. Proc. ACM Symp. Solid Phys. Modeling, 1–12\n(2009). (Best paper award)\n— Geometric inference for measures, F. Chazal, D. Cohen-Steiner and Q.\nMérigot. (submitted)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures | Article Information | J-GLOBAL (https://jglobal.jst.go.jp/en/detail?JGLOBAL_ID=202502208714289434)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; # Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures ... To check the article posted on the arXiv has a history of withdrawal, see the primary information . ... ,  System and control theory in general\n\nPreprint\n\nJ-GLOBAL ID：202502208714289434   Reference number：25P0322450\n\n# Quantitative stability for the Brascamp-Lieb inequality and moment measures\n\nBrascamp-Lieb不等式に対する定量的安定性とモーメント測度〖JST機械翻訳〗\n\nThis is a preprint. See \"About preprints\" page for more details.\n\nTo check the article posted on the arXiv has a history of withdrawal, see the primary information .\n\nAuthor (2)：\n\nMachado Joao Miguel\n\n\nRamos Joao P. G.\n\nAbout Ramos Joao P. G.\n\n  * Search \"202650001263527340\"\n  * S